WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.430
“Your salary is on hold — confirm

2
00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.390
within twenty-four hours.”

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:06.530
Hmm.

4
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.080
My salary, on hold, by text message?

5
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.290
Who writes like that?

6
00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.910
Today: how to spot a phishing message —

7
00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.310
and what to do when one arrives.

8
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.400
Phishing is a message that pretends to

9
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.780
come from someone you trust — a bank,

10
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.280
the ministry, even your head teacher —

11
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.840
to trick you into clicking, paying or

12
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.700
sharing a secret.

13
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.380
It can come by text, email,

14
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.880
chat apps or a phone call.

15
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.860
Look for five warning signs.

16
00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.410
Urgency or threats.

17
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.040
A link or file you didn’t expect.

18
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.250
A sender that doesn’t match.

19
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.060
A request for your PIN, a

20
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.120
one-time code or bank details.

21
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.760
And a prize that’s too good to be true.

22
00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.130
So here’s the habit.

23
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.070
If a message pushes you to act fast, stop.

24
00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.740
Check through another channel — call the

25
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.000
person on a number you already have, or

26
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.620
open the official app yourself.

27
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.730
It’s like a stranger at the school

28
00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.050
gate saying, “The head teacher sent

29
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.310
me for the exam papers.”

30
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.310
You wouldn’t hand them over.

31
00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:20.750
You’d walk to the office and ask.

32
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.550
That walk is checking another way.

33
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.240
Quick check!

34
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.570
A text says my bank card is blocked

35
00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.870
and gives a link to unlock it.

36
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.600
What should I do first?

37
00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.810
Call the bank on the number printed on

38
00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.130
your card — not the number in the message.

39
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.900
A real problem will still be

40
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.460
there after one phone call.

41
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.590
Scams come in many shapes.

42
00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.430
“Pay the exam fee today or

43
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.450
lose your place.”

44
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.700
A caller who sent money “by mistake”

45
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.280
and wants the code you just got.

46
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.640
“You’ve won a laptop — just pay delivery.”

47
00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.910
A file called exam results from

48
00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:01.970
a number you don’t know.

49
00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:03.930
Spotted one?

50
00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.620
Report it with the app’s report button,

51
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.940
warn your school, then delete it.

52
00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:10.270
Clicked by mistake?

53
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:11.520
Don’t panic.

54
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:12.880
Close the page.

55
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.100
If you typed a password, change it now.

56
00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.190
If you shared bank details, call

57
00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.370
your bank straight away.

58
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.320
Your turn.

59
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.580
A colleague’s number writes: “I sent you a

60
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.220
code by mistake — please forward it.”

61
00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.030
Scam or safe?

62
00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.670
Scam — even from a friend’s number,

63
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.850
because accounts get taken over.

64
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.390
Remember: spot the sign, stop, check

65
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.110
another way, then report and delete.

66
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.710
Try it in class tomorrow: write

67
00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:50.750
six short messages on paper.

68
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.210
Pupils sort them into scam or safe

69
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.070
— and point to the warning sign

70
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.450
that gave each scam away.
